hypotheses.statement:
The spaces $J$ and $X$ are reflexive, and finite coordinate arrays are dense in both. If a set $B\subset T$ meets each segment in either a segment or the empty set, its coordinate projection $P_B$ is contractive on both spaces. For an antichain $V$, the coordinate space supported on $V$ has its $\ell_2(V)$ norm in both spaces. In particular, $$\label{dir:atomic:atom-bound}
 \left\|\sum_{I\in\mathcal I}\lambda_I s_I^*\right\|_X
 \le\left(\sum_{I\in\mathcal I}\lambda_I^2\right)^{1/2}$$ for every disjoint segment family. Call a vector on the left with $\sum_I\lambda_I^2\le1$ an atom. The unit ball of $X$ is the norm-closed convex hull of the atoms. If $S$ is finite and ancestral, the unit ball of $P_SX$ is the convex hull of atoms whose segments lie in $S$; every point has a finite convex representation.

Let $X$ have a specified coordinate system with dense finite-support vectors. Fix a family $\mathcal P$ of bounded finite-rank coordinate projections such that every finite support is contained in the range of some $P\in\mathcal P$. Consider separately the following two hypotheses, each imposed for every $P\in\mathcal P$, all pure head and tail vectors $p=Pp$, $Py=0$, and every $R>0$: $$\begin{aligned}
 \|y\|^2&\le C^2 R(R-\|p\|)
 &&\text{if }\|p\pm y\|\le R, \label{met:radial-input}\\
 \|p\|^2+a\|y\|^2&\le R^2
 &&\text{if }\|p\pm y\|\le R. \label{met:square-input}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $C>0$, respectively $a>0$, is uniform over $\mathcal P$, the vectors, and $R$. No uniform bound on $\|P\|$ or $\|I-P\|$ is assumed. For $R>0$ and $\varepsilon>0$, an infinite $\varepsilon$-separated family in the symmetric $R$-lens at $x$ then satisfies, respectively, $$\begin{aligned}
 \varepsilon^2/4&\le C^2R(R-\|x\|),\label{met:radial-lens}\\
 \|x\|^2+a\varepsilon^2/4&\le R^2.\label{met:square-lens}
\end{aligned}$$

Let $R>0$ and put $t=R/4$. Suppose $\gamma>0$ is such that every unit $u$ admits a closed finite-codimensional subspace $F_u$ with $$\label{met:bridge:local-amuc-input}
 \max_\pm\|u\pm t y\|\ge1+\gamma
 \qquad(y\in F_u,\ \|y\|=1).$$ Then $\beta=\gamma/2$ excludes infinite $R$-separated families in $M(u,\beta)$, uniformly over unit $u$.

For $X_{\mathrm v}$ one has $$\label{met:f:sixth-input}
 S-2\|x\|_{\mathrm v}^2
 \ge c_6S^{-2}\|y\|_{\mathrm v}^6,
 \qquad
 S=\|x+y\|_{\mathrm v}^2+\|x-y\|_{\mathrm v}^2,
 \qquad c_6=2\exp(-(\mathrm e-1))4^{-6},$$ whenever $x$ is supported on a finite head and $y$ vanishes there. The right side is interpreted as zero when $S=0$. Every distortion-$D$ embedding of $\mathcal D_k$ into this norm satisfies $$\label{met:f:sixth-power-depth}
 \frac{c_6 k}{8}\le D^6-D^4.$$ For $0<\varepsilon\le1$, put $$\label{met:f:sixth-q}
 q_6(\varepsilon)=
 \left(1-\frac{c_6\varepsilon^6}{512}\right)^{1/2}.$$ Every infinite family satisfying $$\|x+u_j\|_{\mathrm v},\ \|x-u_j\|_{\mathrm v}\le1,
 \qquad \|u_j-u_l\|_{\mathrm v}\ge\varepsilon\quad(j\ne l)$$ has $\|x\|_{\mathrm v}\le q_6(\varepsilon)$. Keeping a fixed unit-lens contraction gives the additional form $$\label{met:f:sixth-depth}
 D^{-1}\le q_6(D^{-1})^k.$$

The dual $X_{\mathrm v}^*$ is separable, and under the same finite-head support conditions as above one has $$\label{met:f:cubic-input}
 \|x\pm y\|_{\mathrm v}\le R
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 R^2\ge\|x\|_{\mathrm v}^2+
       \frac{c_3}{2\sqrt2\,R}\|y\|_{\mathrm v}^3,
 \qquad R>0,\quad c_3=(6\mathrm e)^{-3}.$$ Consequently every distortion-$D$ embedding of $\mathcal D_k$ satisfies $$\label{met:f:cubic-power-depth}
 \frac{c_3 k}{2\sqrt2}\le D^3-D.$$ For $0<\varepsilon\le1$, define $$\label{met:f:cubic-theta}
 \theta(\varepsilon)=
 \left(1-\frac{c_3\varepsilon^3}{2\sqrt2}\right)^{1/2}.$$ If $(h_j)$ is weakly null, $\|h_j\|_{\mathrm v}\ge\varepsilon$, and $\|x\pm h_j\|_{\mathrm v}\le1$, then $\|x\|_{\mathrm v}\le\theta(\varepsilon)$. Every distortion-$D$ embedding of the depth-$k$ countably branching diamond into $X_{\mathrm v}$ satisfies $$\label{met:f:cubic-depth}
 1\le D\theta(D^{-1})^k.$$

